Q: How to create &quot;intermediates&quot; property for the computeRoutes method? A: <p>JSON is Javascript Object Notation. Let's try your JSON value under Javascript:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>console.log([
        {
          "location":{
            "latLng":{
              "latitude": 51.4903,
              "longitude": -0.138728
            },
            "latLng":{
              "latitude": 51.4903,
              "longitude": -0.138728
            }
          }

        }
])</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>As you can see from the snippet, <code>location</code> will have a single value. Why? Because a data member of an object is identified by its name. Hence there can be maximum one <code>latLng</code> member inside <code>location</code>. This means that you can duplicate, triplicate or nplicate the same member name into one object, the only thing you achieve is that the last value by the same name inside the same object will manifest over the others.</p>
<p>Hence, this structure is invalid. You could do something like this instead:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>console.log([
        {
          "location":[
          {
            "latLng":{
              "latitude": 51.4903,
              "longitude": -0.138728
            },
          },
          {
            "latLng":{
              "latitude": 51.4903,
              "longitude": -0.138728
            }
          }
          ]

        }
])</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>which will wrap your location entries into an array. If you really want to have an object instead of the array, then you can do something like</p>
<pre><code>json_encode([&quot;location&quot; =&gt; [
    1 =&gt; $latLng1,
    2 =&gt; $latLng2
]])
</code></pre>
<p>which, not being indexed from 0 will force it to be an object.</p>

Q: Configuration generator panel not taking full width on narrow screens A: <p>Your problem lies in <code>config_generator.js</code> where you set the width of <code>leftPanel</code> when the window resizes. This overrides the rule that sets the width to 100%.</p>
<p><a href="https://github.com/gemprf/gemprf.github.io/blob/d555375b94722c0ce671d623d58128682d128a30/gemprf-configs/js/config_generator.js#L874-L883" rel="nofollow noreferrer">config_generator.js#L874-L883:</a></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const initWidths = () =&gt; {
    const rect = container.getBoundingClientRect();
    const dW = dividerWidth();
    const half = Math.max(minLeft, Math.floor((rect.width - dW) / 2));
    left.style.width = half + 'px';
    right.style.width = (rect.width - half - dW) + 'px';
};
initWidths();
window.recalculatePanels = initWidths;
window.addEventListener('resize', initWidths);
</code></pre>

Q: how to use adoptedStyleSheets to append multiple stylesheets A: <p>It did not work because <code>sheet.insertRule()</code> does insert <em>rule</em>, not a <em>sheet</em>.</p>
<p>Names of the instance, method and property are helpful.<br />
<strong>Sheet</strong>, <strong>Sheets</strong>, <strong>rule</strong></p>
<p>rule/ruleset:</p>
<pre><code>.test {
   background-color: #fff;
}
</code></pre>
<p>multiple rules = sheet</p>
<p>In this example, create one <strong>Sheet</strong> <code>sheet</code>, replace its content with <code>syles1</code>, add two rules into this <code>sheet</code>, insert this sheet into array adoptedStyleSheets.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var styles1 = `
  .test {
    background-color: blue;
  }
  .wow {
    border: 2px solid red;
  }
`;
var rule2a = `
  .test2 {
    background-color: green;
  }
  `;
var rule2b = `
  .wow2 {
    border: 2px solid red;
  }
`;

const sheet = new CSSStyleSheet();
sheet.replaceSync(styles1);

sheet.insertRule(rule2a);
sheet.insertRule(rule2b);

document.adoptedStyleSheets = [...document.adoptedStyleSheets, sheet];</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class="test"&gt;
  TEST
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="wow"&gt;
  WOW
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="test2"&gt;
  TEST2
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="wow2"&gt;
  WOW2
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>or create two <strong>Sheet</strong> instances, <code>sheet1</code> and <code>sheet2</code>, replace content of each with <code>syles1</code> and <code>style2</code> respectively, insert both of <strong>Sheet</strong> instances into array adoptedStyleSheets.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var styles1 = `
  .test {
    background-color: blue;
  }
  .wow {
    border: 2px solid red;
  }
`;
var styles2 = `
  .test2 {
    background-color: green;
  }
  .wow2 {
    border: 2px solid red;
  }
`;

const sheet1 = new CSSStyleSheet();
sheet1.replaceSync(styles1);
const sheet2 = new CSSStyleSheet();
sheet2.replaceSync(styles2);

document.adoptedStyleSheets = [...document.adoptedStyleSheets, sheet1, sheet2];</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class="test"&gt;
  TEST
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="wow"&gt;
  WOW
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="test2"&gt;
  TEST2
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="wow2"&gt;
  WOW2
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
